facebook ceo mark zuckerberg is  responding to what can fairly be  described as scathing testimony from a  former facebook employee who called the  company morally bankrupt whistleblower  francis haugen told the senate panel  yesterday that facebook's own research  shows that it profits from stoking anger  and division also that instagram can be  harmful to teenagers in a facebook post  last night zuckerberg defended his  company writing at the heart of these  allegations  is this idea that we prioritize profit  over safety and well-being that's just  not true  joining us now is facebook's global head  of safety and tickety davis uh antigone  good morning to you thank you for being  here  consequential morning  mark zuckerberg says the company is  being  mischaracterized and let's go to the  heart of the allegation which is that  what's good for facebook is bad for teen  girls bad for democracy and bad for  america what does the company say to  that  well i i think fundamentally that the  characterization of the research and of  the work that we do was j was just off  i've worked at facebook for seven years  we have 40 000 people dedicated to these  issues we have we spent 13 billion  dollars in the last five years on these  issues we care deeply about it and  people do  are safe and secure on our on our  platform  antigone i think the question is about  the business model though and just to  put that 13 billion dollar investment in  context that's what you've spent on  safety and security that is a single  digit fraction of what the company has  taken in over that time period and you  spend a lot more on marketing and sales  so that's the core business concern  and and to that point when you guys talk  to investors and make financial filings  to the federal government like your 10k  report here you talk about engagement  being the top priority engagement and  user growth are you telling me now that  you're amending that that that's no  longer the priority  well let me touch on the business model  because i think that there's it's a very  short-sighted um description of our of  how we do business we put people first  we want people to have a positive  experience on our platform if we build a  platform that is filled with hate that  is not going to actually keep people  interested and it's not something people  are going to want to come to people come  to facebook to share with their photos  of their family and friends they come to  facebook to build their small business  if if it's filled with hate they're not  going to do that and our advertisers  aren't going to come back either  but antigone respectfully all the  available research publicly and what we  know about the research you guys have  internally is that engagement which is  the key business for facebook comes  primarily and most easily from  misinformation divisive content to give  you just one example nyu researchers  found that misinformation recently on  facebook gets six times the engagement  than accurate information so how can you  say that you don't profit off of some of  this harmful content  so the way our algorithm works in your  news feed is based on the things that  are of interest to you so what the  things that you engage with that said we  actually also have  within our systems things that actually  suppress things like click bait things  that are divisive so i think that just  fundamentally is a  um simplification of the way that our  the way that our news feed works  this is key here because when you talk  to investors and you file things with  the sec you say in fact one of the key  risks to your business is anything that  deprioritizes  engagement and user growth in the short  term  are you amending this now are you saying  that your algorithm is going to  prioritize good feelings first  not  engagement by any way  actually to give you to give you an  example of of the work that we've done  we made some adjustments to our  algorithm to create uh to get people  engaged in more meaningful social  interactions and we did that knowing  that it would reduce the amount of time  that people spent on our platform it did  in fact do that by 50 million hours a  day but in the long term that's a better  experience and that's actually better  for business  so just so people are crystal clear here  though what is better financially for  facebook if i spend an extra 30 minutes  of happy engagement or if i spend an  extra two hours in a bitter political  argument  i would say the first for sure  antigone um you speak of this algorithm  and i wanted to read what teen girls  told facebook in a survey and this is in  a disclosure halgen gave to congress  quote i keep looking at these images  after getting off instagram i don't know  sometimes i just don't eat or try to eat  less  i wish i had the money to get my nose or  other body parts fixed  when i keep looking at these pictures i  feel like i am too big  or not pretty enough  so what do you say to parents  like myself raising an 11 year old  daughter who has to wonder  is her spirit  her self-esteem being fractured every  single time she logs on  well i'm also a parent of raise a  teenage daughter and and have been a  teenage girl and actually have taught  middle school and teenage girls and  there is a is a  it is a hard place to be a teenager when  it comes to body image in in in this  world in particular  in the united states and we did this  research to try to understand where it  is that we may see that intersection of  a societal issue on our platform and  what can we do better  so for example we actually if someone  searches for eating disorder content on  our platform we actually uh pop up  resources to help them get support we  don't allow the promotion of that kind  of content on our on our on our platform  we work with a set of eating disorder  experts to help us figure out how to do  this best and what's really interesting  in that research which doesn't didn't  get talked about is that many teens  actually find in fact two out of the of  the  the  out of three um actually find that they  are able to uh  get more more help or bet or their  support through their engagement on on  instagram and what we want to do is  understand the difference between the  people who are who come to the platform  suffering and find it worse from those  who find it better and i'll give you an  example of a very specific thing that  teens actually uh shared with us so they  indicated that that when they see  content about somebody's journey to  recovery or uplifting content that  actually can be helpful to them so we're  presently working on something called  nudges where we would if we see someone  perhaps going down a a path that that is  negative a negative experience we  actually will nudge them towards that  uplifting content  you know antigone uh listen no one  watching francis uh hogan speak  would think that facebook would say god  i'm glad she's speaking up thank you so  much we appreciate what you're saying  here  but i do wonder if all of you at  facebook thought that she raised any  issues that made you think we need to  reconsider how we do some things francis  haugen was very credible she seemed  seemed very powerful seemed to know  exactly what she was talking about she  came uh she told 60 minutes and scott  pelly that she came with the kids called  receipts she made copies of over 10 000  pieces of of evidence because she said  she wanted to make sure that nobody  could refute or dispute what she was  saying so my question is did she raise  any issues that has you all thinking we  need to do things a little differently  around here  well i actually work on the teams that  care about these particular issues and  those are the teams that are actually  doing that research and looking at these  issues we care very much about people  having a positive experience people  feeling safe and and actually uh they're  well being being supported on our  platform so the issues that that that  she raised are actually issues that we  talk about internally to come up with  product changes so all this is done as  directional research to identify how we  can make sure that people are having a  positive experience and and she made it  clear though antigone that what you're  doing is not enough  what you're doing is not enough she even  made a point of saying look i don't  think mark zuckerberg is a bad guy i  don't think he won he intended to hurt  people but what you're doing is  absolutely according to her and the  research that and the evidence that she  presented is not enough  well what struck me actually about about  the hearing and about the presentation  is she showed  all these different documents and then  she never spoke to the product changes  and the product things that we've done  in relation to those documents and that  was actually what was incredibly  disheartening to me and to the thousands  of people who work on this at facebook  there's a different story to tell if you  look at the product changes so i'll give  another example of this there was  evidence given around suicide and  and suicide prevention and issues of  people who come to our platform who are  struggling with those issues facebook  has invested more than any other tech  company in this issue more than that  we've actually put together a a  reporting flow for people who  dedicated to this issue if you go to  report this content you can immediately  connect with someone who's a crisis  response provider  clearly we need more time for this i  know everybody wants regulation  including facebook we'll see and we want  to know what that regulation would be  it's not no one is saying that facebook  has malevolent malevolent actors inside  or is doing nothing it's the question is  is the business model itself  fundamentally a problem and does that  need to change  we hear you on the on the subject we  wait to hear more from congress and we'd  love to have you back and tiggity davis  thank you very much  thank you

